{"lines": ["UVB-76 is a shortwave station on 4625 kilohertz, nicknamed \"the Buzzer.\"", "It broadcasts a short, monotonous buzz about twenty-five times a minute, twenty-four hours a day.", "Radio hobbyists first noticed it in the Soviet era, and the earliest preserved recording dates to 1982.", "Each buzz lasts about a second, then a pause, then another, around the clock.", "It has run through the Soviet collapse, and through every year since.", "Sometimes the buzz stops, and a live voice reads out a message in Russian.", "The messages follow fixed formats, with names like Monolith and Uzor, full of callsigns, code words, and strings of digits.", "From 1997 to 2010 the station called itself UZB-76, so the famous name is actually a mistranscription.", "People call it Doomsday Radio, though it is not linked to the Dead Hand system, but it has been interrupted many times and no missiles have followed.", "The best-supported theory is a military command network for the Leningrad Military District.", "Its true purpose has never been revealed.", "The Buzzer talks to nobody we can see. The next station talked to spies."], "words": [["UVB-76", 0.0, 1.04], ["is", 1.04, 1.82], ["a", 1.82, 1.92], ["shortwave", 1.92, 2.4], ["station", 2.4, 2.74], ["on", 2.74, 3.22], ["4625", 3.22, 4.88], ["kilohertz,", 4.88, 6.54], ["nicknamed", 6.54, 6.9], ["\"the", 6.9, 7.68], ["Buzzer.\"", 7.68, 8.16], ["It", 8.62, 8.67], ["broadcasts", 8.67, 9.0], ["a", 9.0, 9.46], ["short,", 9.46, 9.72], ["monotonous", 9.72, 10.7], ["buzz", 10.7, 10.96], ["about", 10.96, 11.44], ["twenty-five", 11.44, 11.84], ["times", 11.84, 12.38], ["a", 12.38, 12.62], ["minute,", 12.62, 12.78], ["twenty-four", 12.78, 13.54], ["hours", 13.54, 14.1], ["a", 14.1, 14.34], ["day.", 14.34, 14.56], ["Radio", 15.02, 15.36], ["hobbyists", 15.36, 15.98], ["first", 15.98, 16.26], ["noticed", 16.26, 16.64], ["it", 16.64, 16.98], ["in", 16.98, 17.32], ["the", 17.32, 17.44], ["Soviet", 17.44, 17.84], ["era,", 17.84, 18.26], ["and", 1